1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,160
By now you know collectors, leaks, dumps,
escape analysis, and memory beyond the
heap.

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:19,480
So someone pastes a twenty-flag command
line into your deployment manifest and
says "this is how we tune Java." That
paste is not a strategy. It is an
heirloom. Nobody remembers which incident
invented half the flags.

3
00:00:19,599 --> 00:00:32,320
Nobody knows which ones still matter on
the current JDK. Treat flags as
experiments: change one variable, measure
against SLOs, document why the flag
exists, and delete it when it stops
earning its keep.

4
00:00:32,439 --> 00:00:47,039
Begin with intention, not folklore. Set
the heap on purpose. A stable sizing story
— often -Xms equal to -Xmx for server
processes that should not spend time
resizing — is a decision you can explain
in a design review.

5
00:00:47,159 --> 00:00:59,000
Pair it with a collector you chose for
reasons from Episode Fifty-Six, and turn
on GC logging: Look at this bash for a
moment. That line is not "fully tuned." It
is observable.

6
00:00:59,119 --> 00:01:06,599
Without logs and metrics under realistic
load, the rest of the flag list is costume
jewelry. Prefer evidence over folklore.

7
00:01:06,719 --> 00:01:22,439
If p99 fails under load, read GC pauses,
allocation rate, heap occupancy, CPU
steal, and queue depth. Form a hypothesis
that names a mechanism. Change one thing.
Remeasure. That loop is tuning.

8
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:36,719
Copying a blog's ZGC flags because the
title said "ultimate performance" is
cosplay. Make the experiment loop tactile.
Baseline: p99 is 180ms under a load test
that mirrors production mix.

9
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:52,640
GC log shows mixed collections with 40ms
pauses. Hypothesis: heap too small for the
live set plus allocation burst. Change:
raise -Xmx/-Xms together by a measured
step, keep the collector constant.
Remeasure.

10
00:01:52,760 --> 00:02:03,560
If p99 drops and pauses calm, you learned
something. If nothing changes, revert and
form a new hypothesis — maybe latency is
lock contention, and JFR would have said
so.

11
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:15,640
Changing five flags at once teaches
nothing either way. Document why a flag
exists. Future you will not remember that
-XX:SomethingObscure was added during a
Black Friday incident three years ago.

12
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:25,880
A comment in the manifest, a runbook line,
a pull-request note — anything that ties
flag to symptom and proof. Remove flags
that no longer earn their keep.

13
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:36,480
Debug flags, ultra-verbose logging, or
experimental options left on forever
create overhead, noise, and false
confidence. Separate supportability flags
from performance flags.

14
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:49,880
GC logging and JFR configuration often
stay because they make the next incident
cheaper. Random micro-tuning flags must
continually earn their keep against SLOs.
Cargo-cult lists fail predictably.

15
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:00,920
A flag that helped a four-gigabyte heap
may hurt a five-hundred-megabyte
container. A flag that helped Java 11 may
be default, renamed, or irrelevant on Java
21.

16
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:05,720
A flag that reduced pauses may increase
CPU until autoscaling costs explode.

17
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:20,360
Folklore has favorite spells:
-XX:+AggressiveOpts from an ancient blog,
huge thread stacks from a desktop recipe,
GC ratio flags that do not match your
collector, -Xlog:gc*=debug left on until
disks fill.

18
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:33,880
Each fails the documentation test: no
symptom, no measurement, no revert plan.
Prefer a short, owned flag list to a long,
inherited one. This episode does not
memorize every -XX.

19
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:42,600
The catalog is large and version-
dependent. What transfers is discipline:
intentional heap, visible GC behavior,
evidence, documentation, cleanup.

20
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:53,200
When someone suggests a flag, ask "what
symptom?", "what measurement changed?",
and "how do we know to revert?" Missing
answers mean the flag is a rumor with a
dash prefix.

21
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:56,640
We turned flags from magic words into a
scientific habit.

22
00:03:56,760 --> 00:04:09,600
Zoom in further: sometimes the "memory
problem" is not GC policy but the shape of
objects themselves — headers, padding,
compressed oops — and why a "tiny" object
is never as tiny as its fields suggest.
